    "knowledge_point": "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections",
    "problem": "Ann has two shuffled $52$-card decks of playing cards, $A$ and $B.$ $2$ red cards of deck $B$ were lost. The cards of the two decks are mixed together. What is the probability that a randomly selected card from the resulting set of cards is red?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck contains $26$ red cards and $26$ black cards.*",
    "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event that the selected card is red, and let $A$ be the event that the card was one of the cards of deck $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the card was one of the cards of deck $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected card is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red cards in deck $A$ $(26)$ and divide by the total number of cards in both decks $(52+50=102).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{26}{102}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(R\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of red cards in deck $B$ $(24)$ and divide by the total number of cards in both decks $(102).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{24}{102}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected card from either deck is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{102}+\\frac{24}{102} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
